.TH sss 1 "2026-05-09" "sss 2.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sss \- Secret String Substitution: transparent encryption for text files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sss
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ ARGS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sss
is a command-line tool for transparent encryption and decryption of text within files using XChaCha20-Poly1305 with a modern multi-user architecture. It enables seamless protection of sensitive data embedded in configuration files, scripts, and other text documents.
.PP
sss uses a hybrid cryptographic approach combining asymmetric (Ed25519) and symmetric (XChaCha20-Poly1305) cryptography. Each user has a keypair, and file content is encrypted with shared project keys that are individually sealed for each authorised user.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Project Management
.TP
.B sss init \fR[\fIusername\fR] [\fB--crypto\fR \fIclassic|hybrid\fR]
Initialise a new sss project in the current directory. Creates a
.I .sss.toml
configuration file and sets up the initial user. If username is not provided, uses the configured default username or prompts for one. With
.B --crypto hybrid
the project is created at version "2.0" with the hybrid post-quantum suite enabled (requires sss built with
.B --features hybrid
for subsequent operations). Default is
.B --crypto hybrid
(project version "2.0"); pass
.B --crypto classic
for v1.0 behaviour.
.TP
.B sss status
Check if the current directory is within an sss project. Outputs the project root path and exits with status 0 if in a project, 1 otherwise.
.TP
.B sss project users list
List all users configured in the current project, showing their usernames and public key fingerprints.
.TP
.B sss project users add \fIusername\fR \fIpubkey\fR
Add a new user to the project. The public key can be provided as base64-encoded text or as a file path. The project key will be sealed for the new user.
.TP
.B sss project users remove \fIusername\fR
Remove a user from the project. This triggers automatic key rotation to ensure the removed user cannot access future secrets.
.TP
.B sss project users info \fIusername\fR
Display detailed information about a specific project user, including their public key and fingerprint.
.SS Key Management
.TP
.B sss keys generate \fR[\fB--suite\fR \fIclassic|hybrid|both\fR] [\fB--force\fR] [\fB--no-password\fR]
Generate a new keypair. The private key is encrypted with a passphrase (unless
.B --no-password
is specified) and stored in the local keystore. Use
.B --force
to overwrite an existing keypair with the same name.
.B --suite classic
generates an X25519 (classic) keypair only.
.B --suite hybrid
generates an X448 + sntrup761 (trelis) hybrid keypair only.
.B --suite both
generates both classic and hybrid keypairs atomically, using the same Argon2id passphrase-wrap for each private key.
.TP
.B sss keys list
List all keypairs stored in the local keystore, showing their names, IDs, and creation dates.
.TP
.B sss keys pubkey \fR[\fB--fingerprint\fR] [\fB--user\fR \fIusername\fR]
Display the public key of the current keypair, or of a specific project user if
.B --user
is specified. With
.B --fingerprint\fR,
shows the SHA256 fingerprint with visual randomart for verification.
.TP
.B sss keys current \fR[\fIname\fR]
Show the currently active keypair. If a name is provided, sets that keypair as current.
.TP
.B sss keys delete \fIname\fR
Delete a keypair from the local keystore by name or ID. This operation cannot be undone.
.TP
.B sss keys rotate \fR[\fB--force\fR] [\fB--no-backup\fR] [\fB--dry-run\fR]
Rotate the project encryption key. This generates a new symmetric key, re-encrypts all secrets, and updates the project creation timestamp. Use
.B --dry-run
to preview changes without applying them.
.TP
.B sss keys set-passphrase \fIkey-id\fR
Set or change the passphrase that wraps a stored private key. Accepts a full or partial key ID. Prompts twice for the new passphrase (or reads from
.B SSS_PASSPHRASE
under
.B --non-interactive\fR).
.TP
.B sss keys remove-passphrase \fIkey-id\fR
Remove passphrase protection from a stored private key, leaving it unencrypted on disk. Accepts a full or partial key ID. The key remains in the keystore but is no longer wrapped under a passphrase.
.TP
.B sss keys show
Display the public key fingerprint and randomart for every suite present in the current keypair (classic, hybrid, or both). Use this to obtain a hybrid public key string for
.B sss users add-hybrid-key\fR.
.TP
.B sss keygen \fR[\fB--suite\fR \fIclassic|hybrid|both\fR] [\fB--force\fR] [\fB--no-password\fR]
.I (Deprecated: use "sss keys generate" instead)
Generate a new keypair. Accepts the same
.B --suite
selector as
.B sss keys generate
(classic, hybrid, or both).
.SS User Management
The
.B sss users
subcommand offers a top-level alias for the project-user operations also available under
.BR sss\ project\ users .
The two forms are equivalent and may be used interchangeably; the top-level form is preferred when scripting against v2.0 (it is the form returned by
.BR --help ).
.TP
.B sss users list
List project users (alias of
.BR sss\ project\ users\ list ).
.TP
.B sss users add \fIusername\fR \fIpubkey\fR
Add a user to the project (alias of
.BR sss\ project\ users\ add ).
.TP
.B sss users remove \fIusername\fR
Remove a user from the project (alias of
.BR sss\ project\ users\ remove ). Triggers automatic key rotation.
.TP
.B sss users info \fIusername\fR
Show information about a project user (alias of
.BR sss\ project\ users\ info ).
.SS Migration (v2.0)
.TP
.B sss migrate \fR[\fB--dry-run\fR]
Upgrade a v1.0 classic project to v2.0 hybrid by re-wrapping the repository key
.I K
for every authorised user under the hybrid KEM (trelis X448 + sntrup761 \(-> BLAKE3 \(-> XChaCha20-Poly1305).
Rewrites the
.B sealed_key
entries in
.I .sss.toml
and bumps
.B version
from
.B "1.0"
to
.B "2.0"\fR.
No sealed file content is touched.
If any user in
.I .sss.toml
lacks a recorded hybrid public key,
.B sss migrate
exits non-zero with the list of affected users and instructs them to run
.B sss keys generate --suite hybrid
followed by
.BR sss\ users\ add-hybrid-key\fR.
.TP
.B sss migrate --dry-run
Print the full migration plan (which users will be re-wrapped, the version bump) and exit without modifying any file.
Running
.B sss migrate --dry-run
produces no changes detectable by
.BR git-status (1)\fR.
.TP
.B sss users add-hybrid-key \fIusername\fR \fIpubkey\fR
Record a user's hybrid public key in
.I .sss.toml\fR.
.I pubkey
is the base64-encoded hybrid public key (1214 bytes raw: X448 public scalar || sntrup761 public key).
This must be done for every user before
.B sss migrate
can re-wrap their entry.
.SS User Settings
.TP
.B sss settings show
Display current user settings, including default username, editor, output preferences, secrets-file conventions, KDF level, and keyring usage.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--username\fR \fIuser\fR
Set the default username for sss operations. Use 'none' to clear.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--editor\fR \fIeditor\fR
Set the preferred text editor for
.B sss edit\fR.
Use 'none' to clear and fall back to $EDITOR or system defaults.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--coloured\fR \fItrue|false\fR
Enable or disable coloured terminal output.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--secrets-filename\fR \fIfilename\fR
Set the default secrets filename used by project-wide commands (e.g.
.IR passwords ,
.IR .secrets ).
Use 'none' to fall back to the built-in default.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--secrets-suffix\fR \fIsuffix\fR
Set the default secrets file suffix (e.g.
.IR .sealed ,
.IR .passwords ).
Use 'none' to fall back to the built-in default.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--kdf-level\fR \fIlevel\fR
Set the default Argon2id security level for newly generated keys. Accepted values: sensitive (most secure, default), moderate (balanced), interactive (fastest), plus the aliases high/medium/low/fast/balanced and 'none' to clear.
.TP
.B sss settings set \fB--use-keyring\fR \fItrue|false\fR
Enable or disable use of the system keyring for private-key storage (macOS Keychain, Windows Credential Manager, Linux Secret Service).
.TP
.B sss settings reset \fB--confirm\fR
Reset all user settings to defaults. Requires
.B --confirm
to prevent accidental resets.
.TP
.B sss settings location
Show the locations of configuration files on the current system.
.SS Project Settings
.TP
.B sss project show
Display settings for the current project including render and open permissions.
.TP
.B sss project list
List all projects that have been configured with specific settings.
.TP
.B sss project enable render
Allow automatic rendering for the current project. Used by git hooks.
.TP
.B sss project disable render
Disallow automatic rendering for the current project.
.TP
.B sss project enable open
Allow automatic opening (decryption to plaintext markers) for the current project. Used by git hooks.
.TP
.B sss project disable open
Disallow automatic opening for the current project.
.TP
.B sss project remove \fR[\fIpath\fR]
Remove project-specific settings. Uses current directory if path not specified.
.TP
.B sss project \fB--render\fR
Shortcut form: enable automatic rendering for the current project. Equivalent to
.BR sss\ project\ enable\ render .
.SS Project Ignore Patterns
.TP
.B sss project ignore add \fIpattern\fR
Add a glob pattern to the project ignore list (e.g.
.IR *.log ,
.IR build/** ).
Project-wide operations such as
.B sss seal --project
skip files that match any ignore pattern.
.TP
.B sss project ignore remove \fIpattern\fR
Remove a glob pattern from the project ignore list. The pattern must match an existing entry exactly.
.TP
.B sss project ignore list
Show all glob patterns currently in the project ignore list.
.SS Project Secrets Filename
.TP
.B sss project secrets-file set \fIfilename\fR
Set a custom secrets filename for this project (e.g.
.IR .env ,
.IR config ).
Used in place of the default
.IR secrets .
.TP
.B sss project secrets-file show
Show the configured secrets filename for this project (or report the default if none is configured).
.TP
.B sss project secrets-file clear
Clear the project-specific secrets filename and revert to the default
.IR secrets .
.SS Git Hooks Management
.TP
.B sss hooks install \fR[\fB--template\fR] [\fB--multiplex\fR]
Install sss git hooks to the current repository's
.I .git/hooks/
directory. Recommended for automatic encryption/decryption during git operations. With
.B --template
the hooks are installed into the user's git template directory (affecting future clones) instead of the current repository. With
.B --multiplex
the hooks are installed into the multiplexed
.I hooks.d/
structure rather than as monolithic scripts.
.TP
.B sss hooks export
Export sss git hooks to
.I ~/.config/sss/hooks/
for manual installation or inspection.
.TP
.B sss hooks list
List all available git hooks embedded in the sss binary.
.TP
.B sss hooks show \fIhook-name\fR
Display the contents of a specific hook (e.g., pre-commit, post-merge, post-checkout).
.SS File Operations
.TP
.B sss seal \fR[\fB-x\fR] [\fB--project\fR] \fIfile\fR
Encrypt plaintext markers (⊕{...} or o+{...}) to ciphertext markers (⊠{...}). By default, outputs to stdout. Use
.B -x
for in-place modification. Use
.B --project
to seal all files in the project (always in-place). Accepts '-' to read from stdin.
.TP
.B sss open \fR[\fB-x\fR] [\fB--project\fR] \fIfile\fR
Decrypt ciphertext markers to plaintext markers. By default, outputs to stdout. Use
.B -x
for in-place modification. Use
.B --project
to open all files in the project (requires permission). Accepts '-' to read from stdin.
.TP
.B sss render \fR[\fB-x\fR] [\fB--project\fR] \fIfile\fR
Decrypt ciphertext markers to raw text (removes all markers). By default, outputs to stdout. Use
.B -x
for in-place modification. Use
.B --project
to render all files in the project (requires permission). Accepts '-' to read from stdin.
.TP
.B sss edit \fIfile\fR
Edit a file with automatic encryption/decryption. Opens the file in the configured editor with markers in plaintext form, then automatically seals it on save. Always modifies in-place.
.SH STRING PATTERNS
sss recognises the following patterns in text files:
.TP
.B ⊕{content}
Plaintext marker (UTF-8). Default output format for decrypted content.
.TP
.B o+{content}
Plaintext marker (ASCII alternative). Useful for systems with limited Unicode support.
.TP
.B ⊠{content}
Ciphertext marker. Contains base64-encoded encrypted data.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Initial Setup
Generate a keypair and initialise a project:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss keys generate
Enter passphrase: ****
$ cd my-project
$ sss init alice
.RE
.fi
.SS Basic Usage
Encrypt a configuration file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ cat config.yaml
database_url: ⊕{postgresql://user:secret@localhost/db}
api_key: ⊕{sk_live_abc123}

$ sss seal -x config.yaml
$ cat config.yaml
database_url: ⊠{base64_encrypted_data...}
api_key: ⊠{base64_encrypted_data...}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Decrypt for viewing:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss open config.yaml
database_url: ⊕{postgresql://user:secret@localhost/db}
api_key: ⊕{sk_live_abc123}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Render to raw text (no markers):
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss render config.yaml
database_url: postgresql://user:secret@localhost/db
api_key: sk_live_abc123
.RE
.fi
.SS Multi-User Workflow
Add a collaborator:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss project users add bob "$(cat bob_pubkey.txt)"
$ sss project users list
alice   SHA256:abc123...
bob     SHA256:def456...
.RE
.fi
.PP
Remove a user (triggers key rotation):
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss project users remove bob
Rotating project key...
Re-encrypting 15 files...
.RE
.fi
.SS Git Integration
Install hooks for automatic processing:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss hooks install
$ sss project enable render
$ sss project enable open

# Now git operations automatically handle encryption
$ git add config.yaml    # pre-commit hook seals secrets
$ git pull              # post-merge hook opens secrets
.RE
.fi
.SS Key Management
View your public key:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss keys pubkey --fingerprint
SHA256:abcdef1234567890...
+---[ED25519]----+
|    . o..       |
|   . = +        |
|  . + B         |
+-----------------+
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rotate project key:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss keys rotate --dry-run
Would re-encrypt 15 files
$ sss keys rotate
Rotating project key...
.RE
.fi
.SS Using stdin
Process secrets via pipes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ echo "API key: ⊕{secret123}" | sss seal -
API key: ⊠{encrypted...}

$ cat encrypted.txt | sss render - > plaintext.txt
.RE
.fi
.SS Settings Management
Configure defaults:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ sss settings set --username alice
$ sss settings set --editor vim
$ sss settings set --coloured true
$ sss settings set --kdf-level moderate
$ sss settings show
.RE
.fi
.SS Hybrid Suite Migration
Create a fresh v2.0 project, or upgrade a v1.0 project from classic to hybrid:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Fresh v2.0 project (no migration needed)
$ sss keys generate --suite both
Enter passphrase: ****
$ sss init --crypto hybrid alice

# Or: migrate an existing v1.0 project to v2.0
$ sss keys generate --suite hybrid
Enter passphrase: ****
$ sss keys show > alice-hybrid.pub      # capture the hybrid public key
$ sss users add-hybrid-key alice "$(cat alice-hybrid.pub)"
$ sss migrate --dry-run
Would re-wrap 3 users: alice, bob, carol
Would bump version: 1.0 -> 2.0
$ sss migrate
Migrated 3 users. .sss.toml version is now 2.0.
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .sss.toml
Project configuration file containing user public keys and sealed project keys.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sss/keystore.toml
Keystore metadata (Linux and BSD).
.TP
.I ~/.config/sss/keys/
Directory containing encrypted private keys (Linux and BSD).
.TP
.I ~/.config/sss/settings.toml
User settings and preferences.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/sss/
Keystore location on macOS.
.TP
.I %APPDATA%\\sss\\
Keystore location on Windows.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
The following options apply to every subcommand and may be passed either before or after the subcommand name:
.TP
.B --confdir \fIDIR\fR
Override the configuration directory. Replaces the platform default (e.g.
.IR ~/.config/sss/ )
for the duration of the invocation. Useful for tests, multi-account workflows, and CI.
.TP
.B --non-interactive
Refuse to prompt for any input. If a passphrase is required it must be supplied via the
.B SSS_PASSPHRASE
environment variable; otherwise the command exits non-zero.
.TP
.B --kdf-level \fILEVEL\fR
Override the Argon2id security level for the current invocation. Accepted values: sensitive (default, most secure), moderate (balanced), interactive (fastest), plus the aliases high, medium, low, fast, and balanced.
.TP
.BR -V ", " --version
Print the version string and exit.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print help for the command being invoked and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SSS_USER
Override the default username for SSS operations. Takes precedence over configured default.
.TP
.B SSS_PASSPHRASE
Supplies the keypair passphrase non-interactively. Required when
.B --non-interactive
is in effect and the operation needs to unwrap a private key. Cleared from process memory after use.
.TP
.B EDITOR
Fallback text editor if not configured via
.B sss settings set --editor\fR.
.TP
.B VISUAL
Secondary fallback for text editor selection.
.SH HYBRID SUITE
.PP
sss v2.0 introduces an opt-in post-quantum cryptographic suite for per-user repository-key
wrapping, alongside the classic libsodium suite.
.PP
.B WARNING:
The hybrid suite depends on
.B trelis
(X448 + sntrup761), which is
.B experimental and has not been formally audited\fR.
The classic suite (libsodium, v1.0) remains the recommended default for all production
deployments until a trelis audit is completed.
.PP
The suite in use is determined by the
.B version
field in
.IR .sss.toml :
.TP
.B version = "1.0"
Classic suite. Repo-key wrapping via libsodium
.BR crypto_box_seal (3)
(X25519 + XSalsa20-Poly1305).
.TP
.B version = "2.0"
Hybrid suite. Repo-key wrapping via trelis KEM (X448 + sntrup761) \(->
BLAKE3 key derivation \(->
XChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD.
.PP
.B The in-file ciphertexts are byte-identical regardless of which suite is in use.
Only the per-user
.B sealed_key
entries in
.I .sss.toml
differ.
.B sss migrate
re-wraps those entries without touching any sealed file.
.SH SECURITY
sss uses XChaCha20-Poly1305 authenticated encryption with deterministic nonce generation for content encryption. Private keys are protected using Argon2id key derivation and stored encrypted in the local keystore.
.PP
Key security properties:
.IP \(bu 2
Deterministic encryption: same plaintext in the same file → same ciphertext → clean git diffs
.IP \(bu 2
No nonce reuse: different plaintexts always get different nonces
.IP \(bu 2
Cross-file protection: same secret in different files → different ciphertexts
.IP \(bu 2
Cross-project protection: different projects → different ciphertexts
.IP \(bu 2
Forward security: key rotation changes both key and timestamp
.PP
Private keys are never shared between users. Project keys are sealed individually for each user using crypto_box_seal (X25519 + XChaCha20-Poly1305).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success
.TP
.B 1
General error (invalid usage, file not found, cryptographic failure, etc.)
